# define your env variables for the test env here
KERNEL_CLASS='App\Kernel'
APP_SECRET='$ecretf0rt3st'

# The test suite talks to its own database server (XIV-10).
#
# `database-test` keeps everything in RAM and runs with fsync off, which is the
# right trade for data thrown away after every run and the wrong one for the dev
# tenants — so they stay on `database`, on disk, untouched by anything the suite
# does.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://app:!ChangeMe!@database-test:5432/app?serverVersion=18&charset=utf8"
TENANT_DSN_TEMPLATE="postgresql://{user}@database-test:5432/{database}?serverVersion=18&charset=utf8"
TENANT_ADMIN_DSN="postgresql://app:!ChangeMe!@database-test:5432/postgres?serverVersion=18&charset=utf8"

# **The suite does not use the mail catcher, and must not start to** (XIV-41).
#
# Mailpit is running — dev points at it — and reading its API would be an easy
# way to assert that something was sent. It is the wrong way. Eight paratest
# workers run at once against one catcher, so its inbox is a single mutable
# thing eight processes would append to and search concurrently: a test would
# find another worker's mail, or delete it, and the failure would be somebody
# else's test on a rerun. That is exactly the collision XIV-9 and XIV-51 spent
# their time removing, and no assertion is worth putting it back.
#
# So mail goes nowhere here, and tests assert on it in memory instead. Symfony's
# message logger collects every message before the transport is reached, which
# is what MailerAssertionsTrait (assertEmailCount, getMailerMessages) reads — per
# process, isolated by construction, and it works precisely because the transport
# below discards.
MAILER_DSN=null://null

###> symfony/panther ###
# The browser tests drive the *test* environment, so the application the browser
# talks to reads this file too — same control plane, same tenant databases, and
# therefore the tenant a test has just provisioned (XIV-31). The recipe's default
# is an environment called `panther`, which this kernel does not allow and which
# would be a third set of databases nobody creates. Reinstalling the package puts
# it back, so check this line if the browser tests start reporting a 500 on every
# page.
PANTHER_APP_ENV=test
PANTHER_ERROR_SCREENSHOT_DIR=./var/error-screenshots
PANTHER_DEVTOOLS=0

# Where the browser is, and the name it reaches this application by. Both are
# compose services; see compose.override.yaml for why the hostname is set twice.
PANTHER_SELENIUM_HOST=http://browser:4444
PANTHER_APP_HOSTNAME=xivi-e2e

# **The web server the browser talks to is `php -S`, which has no TLS** (XIV-105).
#
# The six browser classes that were here before this never notice. The landing
# page does: its routes are `https`-only, so a plain-HTTP request to them is
# answered with a redirect to a port nothing is listening on, and the page is
# unreachable from this layer as shipped.
#
# This router stands in for the TLS terminator production has, and tells the
# front controller that a request to the *signup* host — and only that host —
# arrived securely. Nothing in the application is relaxed; see the file itself,
# which argues the point at length.
#
# Relative to `public/`, which is the working directory Panther starts the server
# in.
PANTHER_WEB_SERVER_ROUTER=../tests/panther-router.php
###< symfony/panther ###

# Self-service signup is **on** in the suite (XIV-64), because the interesting
# assertions are about an endpoint that exists: which host it answers on, what it
# refuses, and what it writes. The switched-*off* state is asserted against
# SignupRouteLoader directly rather than by booting a second kernel — the thing
# being claimed is that the route collection is empty, and that is what the
# loader returns.
#
# **`signup.e2e` rather than `signup.localhost`, and the browser is why**
# (XIV-105). The name check on the landing page is Stimulus, so proving it works
# needs a browser, and a browser can only reach a host-bound page by asking for
# the host it is bound to. Chromium answers every `*.localhost` name from its own
# loopback before DNS is consulted — draft-west-let-localhost-be-localhost — so no
# amount of compose wiring makes `signup.localhost` reachable from the browser
# container. Any other parent resolves the ordinary way, and
# `compose.override.yaml` publishes this one as a second network alias for the
# application container.
#
# The dot is kept because `SignupFirewallTest` needs one: it proves the firewall
# is scoped by a matcher rather than by a regular expression, by asking for the
# same hostname with its dots substituted.
#
# Nothing else in the suite depends on the spelling: every signup test asks
# `SignupHost::normalisedHost()` for it, and the hostname a self-service signup is
# given is derived by `SelfServiceTenantHostname` from the host's parent — `e2e`
# here, where it used to be `localhost`.
SIGNUP_HOST=signup.e2e
XIVI_SIGNUP_SECRET=test-signup-secret

# And the landing page (XIV-65), on for the same reason and asserted off the same
# way: SignupPageTest drives the form, the client and the intake through one real
# request each, while the two switched-off states are asserted against
# SignupRouteLoader — where "off" is a routing table with nothing in it rather
# than a consequence of one.
SIGNUP_PAGE=true

# **MAILER_SENDER is deliberately left unset here**, which is what OutgoingMailTest
# and SendEmailTest are about: §8.7's fallback sends a tenant's mail from
# `no-reply@` at that customer's own primary domain. Signup falls back the same
# way, one noun along — `no-reply@` at SIGNUP_HOST — so both halves are exercised
# by leaving this alone. Setting it here would have quietly rewritten the `From`
# of every tenant mail assertion in the suite.

# **The price list has no currency in the suite, and that is pinned here rather
# than inherited** (XIV-102, XIV-111).
#
# `PriceCurrency` reads `PRICE_CURRENCY`, and two of XIV-102's tests are about
# what an *unset* one renders — a bare number, never a guessed code. They were
# written against `.env`'s empty default and therefore against a developer's
# working copy: setting `PRICE_CURRENCY=CHF` in `.env` to try the pricing screens
# broke both of them, which is a test failing for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the branch under test.
#
# Empty here makes the suite say what it means. A test that wants a currency sets
# one itself, which is the only way it can also assert what the currency does.
PRICE_CURRENCY=
